EZ#128EZ$\tab\tab    \ul EZ\ulnone  is a version-dependent internal command register of TECOC\par
                     which contains various control function bits which can affect\par
                     overall operation.  The value already contained in the \i EZ\ul\i0\par
\ulnone                      register is inclusive-or'ed (\ul #\ulnone ) with 128 then the updated\par
                     value is written back to the \i EZ\i0  register.  By forcing the\par
                     referenced bit to the on state, buffer reads limited by <FF>\par
                     characters are inhibited.  Other TECO implementations may\par
                     handle this situation differently (or not at all); the logic\par
                     flow of the STRIP.TEC macro depends on this operational\par
                     characteristic with respect to searching for <FF> as a\par
                     nondescript character.\par
\par
HXQ\tab\tab\tab    All of the (w\ul H\ulnone ole) text in the edit buffer is copied (\ul X\ulnone )\par
\tab\tab\tab    into Q-register \i Q\i0 .  The referenced text is the passed\par
\tab\tab\tab    complete pathname argument, which is the expansion of the\par
\tab\tab\tab    \i STRIPFILE\i0  environment variable such as:\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    \ul %PDP-8_DRIVE%\\PEPS\\Folders\\Working_Files\\PTPOUT.TXT\ulnone\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    which includes the contents of the environment variable\par
                     \i PDP-8_DRIVE\i0 .  This reduces to something of the form:\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    \ul P:\\PEPS\\Folders\\Working_Files\\PTPOUT.TXT\ulnone .\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    The expanded string is placed in the edit buffer by invoking\par
                     TECOC in this manner.\par
\par
                     Note: The \i STRIPFILE\i0  environment variable is generally derived\par
                     from the \ul %1\ulnone  argument to the batch file initiating the TECOC\par
                     command.  Command-line arguments to invoke TECO variants are\par
                     extremely system-dependent; this method is valid for the\par
                     MS-DOS\super tm\nosupersub  and WINDOWS\super tm\nosupersub  versions of TECOC.\par
\par
HK\tab\tab\tab    Delete (\ul K\ulnone ill) the entire (w\ul H\ulnone ole) passed edit buffer, (now\par
                     saved in Q-register \i Q)\i0 .\page GQ\tab\tab\tab    \ul G\ulnone et the contents of Q-Register \i Q\i0  (back) into the edit buffer.\par
\par
                     Note: This method is used to allow additional file commands\par
                     (perhaps needed in a future version of the STRIP.TEC program).\par
\par
0JIEB$\tab\tab    \ul J\ulnone ump to the beginning (\ul 0\ulnone ) of the buffer and insert the string\par
                     \ul EB\ulnone  there.  The \ul E\ulnone dit \ul B\ulnone ackup command (when fully formed) will be\par
                     executed as a separate TECO macro command.\par
 \par
ZJ27I$\tab\tab    A trailing escape character is required to complete the\par
\tab\tab\tab    macro command before it is executed.  The buffer pointer is\par
\tab\tab\tab    set to point to the end of the edit buffer (\ul ZJ\ulnone ), then an\par
                     <ESC> character is \ul I\ulnone nserted into the edit buffer to complete\par
                     the command contents.\par
\par
HXB\tab\tab\tab    The entire (w\ul H\ulnone ole) edit buffer contents, which is something of\par
                     the form \ul EBP:\\PEPS\\Folders\\Working_Files\\PTPOUT.TXT$\ulnone , is\par
                     copied (\ul X\ulnone ) into Q-register \i B\i0 .\par
\par
HK\tab\tab\tab    \ul K\ulnone ill the entire (w\ul H\ulnone ole) edit buffer (since it is now saved in\par
                     Q-register \i B\i0 ).\par
\par
MB\tab\tab\tab    The text string contents of Q-register \i B\i0  is executed using a\par
                     \ul M\ulnone acro \ul\i B\ulnone\i0  command.\par
\par
                     Note: This sets up the passed file for editing its contents.\par
\par
\b Line 2\b0\tab\tab    \b Y0J<N^X$;-1A&127U1-D\par
\b0\par
Y\tab\tab\tab    The \ul Y\ulnone ank command reads some portion of the input file into the\par
                     edit buffer.  Since <FF> checking has been inhibited, only the\par
                     currently available buffer size determines the exact quantity\par
                     read in at any point.  TECO implementations vary as to exact\par
                     edit buffer size; a number of additional locations are\par
                     generally reserved in case the edit buffer grows larger.\par
\par
                     Note: Typical usage of the STRIP.TEC program will generally\par
                     maintain the overall file size; certain specific operations\par
                     might tend to raise or lower the overall size (slightly)\par
                     depending on specific file contents.  Most character\par
                     processing consists of merely clearing the high-order (mark\par
                     parity) bit.  As such, the overall character count tends to \par
                     remain (relatively) constant.\par
\par
0J\tab\tab\tab    Reset the buffer pointer (\ul J\ulnone ump) to the beginning (\ul 0\ulnone ) of the\par
                     edit buffer; most TECO implementations will do this\par
\tab\tab\tab    automatically after a \ul Y\ulnone ank operation.  However, TECO programs\par
                     will (often) include this to clarify the scope of an iteration\par
                     loop that follows.\par
\par
<\tab\tab\tab    Because of the leading \ul <\ulnone  character, what follows (up to a\par
\tab\tab\tab    matching \ul >\ulnone  character) is executed in an infinite loop.\par
\tab\tab\tab    Various methods exist to break out of the loop, such as a\par
\tab\tab\tab    failing search operation.\par
\par
                     Note: This is the primary operational loop for (most of) the\par
                     STRIP.TEC program.\par
\par
N^X$;\tab\tab\tab    A \ul N\ulnone onstop search is performed; all characters processed will\par
                     be forced into the output buffer and (eventually) written to\par
                     the output file.  In this case, the search is a special-case\par
                     operation indicated by the single character \ul Control-X\ulnone .  This\par
                     means the search argument is any (existent) single character\par
                     of any value.  The only way the search can fail is when the\par
                     search returns a special value indicating there is no further\par
                     input to process.\page                      Note: This condition can be detected by alternate means; the\par
                     value of all "additional" characters is always set to return\par
                     \ul -1\ulnone  at the end of the input file.  The \ul Control-X\ulnone  method is used\par
                     within the STRIP.TEC program because implementation is more\par
                     straight-forward, albeit requiring a fully compliant TECO\par
                     implementation.\par
\par
                     As implemented, this mechanism is not searchable in the\par
                     ordinary sense.  It is merely the special status of the buffer\par
                     pointer being positioned at the end of the input file and not\par
                     an actual character; searching for any input character failed\par
                     to obtain a matching result. \par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    When any form of search inside of iteration brackets fails\par
\tab\tab\tab    and a trailing \ul ;\ulnone  character exists, the normal error abort\par
\tab\tab\tab    is inhibited.  The iteration loop ends and execution\par
\tab\tab\tab    continues past the \ul >\ulnone  character matching the \ul <\ulnone  character in\par
                     effect when the failing search was attempted.  It is quite\par
                     typical to use this method for handling end of file input.\par
\par
                     Note: In STRIP.TEC there is only one general iteration loop.\par
                     TECO allows any number of nested iteration loops as needed,\par
                     including the "breaking out" function if needed.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    Assuming the search succeeded, the buffer pointer is now\par
\tab\tab\tab    just past the matching character.  Should a character be\par
\tab\tab\tab    inserted, the buffer pointer will be placed just past the\par
\tab\tab\tab    newly inserted character.  In some instances the matching\par
\tab\tab\tab    character will be deleted (causing the buffer pointer to be\par
\tab\tab\tab    placed just past the previous character, if any).\line\line                      Note: Character insertion or deletion after a match is only\par
                     performed in certain specific cases.\par
\tab\tab\tab   \par
                     \ul Control-X\ulnone  as used here is the single character \ul Control-X\ulnone\par
                     (ASCII code 24 decimal, 30 octal); in other contexts, the\par
\tab\tab\tab    two-character sequence \ul ^X\ulnone  (or \ul ^x\ulnone ) is also allowed.  However,\par
                     all search arguments in TECO must be stated literally.\par
\par
                     Note: Most TECO implementations echo the \ul Control-X\ulnone  character\par
                     as \ul ^X\ulnone ; when viewing macros such as STRIP.TEC, the correct\par
                     usage must be confirmed as display operations are generally\par
                     anomalous in this regard.\par
\par
                     Since a \ul ;\ulnone  character is present just past the search argument,\par
                     the search can fail without an error message; the iterative\par
                     search loop will end and execution continues just past the \ul >\par
\ulnone                      character as detailed in the line 20 description below.\par
\par
-1A&127U1-D\tab\tab    When the \ul A\ulnone  command is used with a leading numerical\par
\tab\tab\tab    argument, the enumerated value is used as an offset from the\par
\tab\tab\tab    buffer pointer to locate a particular character in the\par
\tab\tab\tab    buffer.  In this instance the numeric value of the previous\par
\tab\tab\tab    character (-1) is returned (which is the character that\par
\tab\tab\tab    matched the search as described above).  This value is then\par
\tab\tab\tab    masked against the constant 127 (177 octal) via a logical\par
\tab\tab\tab    .AND. operation which preserves the low-order seven bits\par
\tab\tab\tab    while removing the high-order bit.  The filtered value is\par
\tab\tab\tab    then stored (U) in numeric Q-register 1 for further testing;\par
\tab\tab\tab    the original character (located one position before (-) the\par
\tab\tab\tab    current buffer pointer value) is then Deleted (D) from the\par
\tab\tab\tab    edit buffer.  (Later in the program, the test character will\par
\tab\tab\tab    generally be inserted into the edit buffer unless special\par
\tab\tab\tab    handling is required.)\page\b Line 3\tab\tab    Q1-26"NONOTEND$'."EONOCHARS$\b0\par
\b\par
\b0 Q1-26"NONOTEND$'\tab    If the test character is not \ul Control-Z\ulnone  (ASCII code 26\par
\tab\tab\tab    decimal, 32 octal), transfer control (using the gOtO (\ul O\ulnone )\par
\tab\tab\tab    command) to the portion of the program labeled \i !NOTEND!\i0 .  If\par
\tab\tab\tab    the character is \ul Control-Z\ulnone , execution continues inline to\par
\tab\tab\tab    finalize the file in the next code section.\par
\par
                     Note: The host operating system may have different conventions\par
                     regarding the end of file state from the operating system that\par
                     created the file; this is why end-of-file detection is also\par
                     carried out elsewhere in the program.\par
\par
                     ASCII text files created by/for OS/8 or the \i P?S/8 SHELL\par
                    \i0  will generally conform to this convention.  Extraneous\par
                     characters may exist in the file past the sentinel Control-Z\par
                     character; all such characters will be ignored.\par
\par
                     Note: The P?S/8 SHELL is an optional feature that will be\par
                     implemented in the next full release, sometime after\par
                     P?S/8 Version 9A is completed.  Consult various P?S/8\par
                     documents (available elsewhere) for more information.\par
\par
                     In extremely rare situations, known defective OS/8 utilities\par
                     may create files that end on the last character of the last\par
                     file record without using a sentinel Control-Z character; this\par
                     situation is correctly handled by the STRIP.TEC program.\par
                     However, it may be necessary to remedy this situation within\par
                     OS/8 to add a proper sentinel Control-Z character.  MS-DOS\super tm\nosupersub\par
                    \super  \nosupersub and Windows\super tm\nosupersub  do not support this convention internally; exact\par
                     byte counts are used to account for the proper file length.\par
                     P?S/8 conversion programs from/to OS/8 include safeguards\par
                     against this problem; \i OS/8 PIP\i0  operating in ASCII text mode\par
                     should be used to correct this problem as required.  \i Kermit-12\i0\par
                     for OS/8 implements internal defensive measures to prevent\par
                     this problem by making this situation impossible to occur.  If\par
                     need be, an entire extra record containing only Control-Z\par
                     characters would be appended at the file ending point.\par
\par
."EONOCHARS$         If the edit buffer pointer is zero, there were no characters\par
\tab\tab\tab    in the buffer when the Control-Z sentinel character was\par
                     detected.  This is not a likely situation; however, this is\par
                     a portion of the "belts and suspenders" approach taken within\par
                     this program.  Should this condition apply, transfer control\par
                     to the portion of the program labeled \i !NOCHARS!\i0  bypassing all\par
                     commands beyond this point in the program.\par
\par
\b Line 4\tab\tab    '-3A-12"NONOFF$'.-3UZQZJ!NOFF!0,.P0,.K!NOCHARS!EFEX$\b0\par
\par
'-3A-12"NONOFF$'     The implementation of the standard OS/8 high-speed\par
                     reader/punch coresident device handlers is quite poor.  The\par
                     reader handler will generally drop the last character; this is\par
                     problematic when the contents of the data stream are not\par
                     delimited by leader/trailer codes.\par
\par
                     Note: It is not clear if the implemented design logic actually\par
                     applies to properly adjusted hardware; it may have merely been\par
                     used as a defensive measure applied by an ignorant programmer\par
                     not aware of various maintenance issues of the PC04/PC05\par
                     reader/punch devices.  This was especially true in the case of\par
                     DEC in-house development systems known to have had shoddy\par
                     maintenance procedures.  In essence, program development was\par
                     often performed on aberrant hardware inconsistent with user\par
                     systems nominally configured with the same devices.\page                      In the \i PEPS/SIMH\i0  environment, the notion of a malformed\par
                     character does not apply; as such, it is necessary to obtain\par
                     every character.  Unfortunately, the handler design is such\par
                     that a double-buffered approach is taken that will predictably\par
                     ignore the final character.\line\line                      To remedy this problem, \i OS/8 PIP\i0  may be used to restore the\par
                     final character (generally a <LF>) or be certain the OS/8\par
                     program that will process the file ignores <LF> characters.\par
                     (\i OS/8 PAL8\i0  delimits source lines with <CR> and ignores <LF>.)\par
\par
                     When using \i P?S/8 OS8CON\i0  to convert OS/8 input to P?S/8 output\par
                     files, this case is properly handled; when converting P?S/8\par
                     text files to OS/8 text files, the result is always properly\par
                     formed with <CR> and <LF> on every line and a Control-Z\par
                     character is always provided as a sentinel character to\par
                     signify proper end-of-file.\par
\par
                     The OS/8 high-speed punch handler situation is far worse:  A\par
                     mis-feature intended only for the Typeset-8 group was foisted\par
                     on the general OS/8 user community despite it having no\par
                     constructive purpose.\par
\par
                     The mis-feature is the deliberate insertion of otherwise\par
                     extraneous characters to attempt to format long paper tapes to\par
                     be placed into the reader stations of offline Teletype Model\par
                     ASR-35 terminals; These ASR-35 terminals were generously\par
                     available in all in-house Typeset-8 installations.  A sensible\par
                     arrangement would have included a Typeset-8-based (or other\par
                     utility program) to properly handle the particulars of this\par
                     terminal as required; instead, the paper tape is greatly\par
                     lengthened because of badly implemented attempts to directly\par
                     format and print the output.\par
\par
                     While this conceptual approach is quite bad, the actual\par
                     implementation is even worse: Proper handling of the <HT>\par
                     character on this device depends on exact carriage position.\par
                     Depending on the particulars, one or perhaps two fill\par
                     characters must be placed into the data stream, allowing the\par
                     file to be self-contained (as apparently was intended).  A\par
                     simple formula \b should\b0  have been used to determine the proper\par
                     fill character count; instead a single <NUL> (or perhaps <RO>)\par
                     character is always inserted just past the <HT> character.\par
                     While this method does work in nearly half of the applicable\par
                     cases that apply, this causes the output to be needlessly\par
                     slowed down where the fill character is not required, and when\par
                     two fill characters are needed, it is inadequate, leading to\par
                     character smear on the terminal paper (as the carriage is\par
                     still in motion when the character is printed).\par
\par
                     Various filler character strategies (both adequate and\par
                     inadequate) are used for the supported control characters,\par
                     which includes <FF>, <VT> and <HT>.  Quite pointlessly,\par
                     distinct fill characters are used in some cases different from\par
                     others.  The approach used is sometimes simple-minded; the\par
                     worst-case quantity of fill characters is used instead of\par
                     using a programmed method that keeps track of where on the\par
                     printed page the current carriage position would be located.\par
                     (Had that better method been used, the typical count of fill\par
                     characters would be far less.)\par
\par
                     Note: The quirky behavior of the standard OS/8 PTP: handler is\par
                     completely by design.  For more information, contact the author\par
                     using the indicated email address; include in the subject line:\par
                     "Typeset-8 Edict".\page                      The STRIP.TEC macro is designed to eliminate every instance of\par
                     undesirable output; as such, a properly formatted output file\par
                     is created suitable for further processing.  As such, there\par
                     are certain quirky cases that must be addressed in this\par
                     section of the program.\par
\par
                     While some of the associated overhead has already been removed\par
                     elsewhere, a specific case has been observed where a <FF>\par
                     followed by <CR>/<LF> is in the buffer just before the\par
                     Control-Z sentinel character.  Since one of the goals of the\par
                     program is to remove trailing <FF> characters, this section\par
                     was added to explicitly remove this undesirable character\par
                     sequence.\par
\par
                     If the last three characters just before the Control-Z\par
                     sentinel character are <FF> <CR> <LF> they will be removed.\par
                     Checking three characters before the Control-Z sentinel\par
                     character, compare that character to <FF>.  If not a match,\par
                     proceed to the next section of the program (labeled \i !NOFF!\i0 ).\par
\par
                     Note: This simplified method is adequate for this purpose.\par
                     The reasoning is that it is not possible for there to be a\par
                     line that does not have both a <CR> and a <LF> at this point\par
                     in the overall filtering process.  As such, the two characters\par
                     after the <FF> are already completely determined and all three\par
                     characters can be removed with impunity.\par
\par
.-3UZQZJ!            If the tested character matches <FF>, store it's position in Q\par
                     register Z; then change the edit buffer pointer to match this \par
                     position effectively ignoring the three undesirable characters.\par
                     (The label \i !NOFF!\i0  follows.)\par
\par
!NOFF!0,.P0,.K\tab    All characters in the edit buffer to this point (0,.) are\par
\tab\tab\tab    output using the Punch (P) command then deleted using the\par
\tab\tab\tab    Kill (K) command.  These operations are skipped if the edit\par
\tab\tab\tab    buffer is empty.  (The label \i !NOCHARS!\i0  follows.)\par
\par
!NOCHARS!EFEX$\tab    The output file is closed using the End File (EF) command;\par
\tab\tab\tab    TECO EXits (EX) to the operating system.\par
\par
\b Line 5\tab\tab    !NOTEND!Q1"EOPL8CH1$'Q1-127"EOIGNORE$'OFFCHK$\b0\par
\par
!NOTEND!\tab\tab    The rest of the program processes the test character\par
\tab\tab\tab    checking for various special cases.  All additional\par
\tab\tab\tab    operations are within the iteration brackets ("<" and ">")\par
\tab\tab\tab    and will be performed repeatedly.  Each iteration starts\par
\tab\tab\tab    with the Non-stop search for any existent character until\par
\tab\tab\tab    failing due to lack of further input.  (Note: All processing\par
\tab\tab\tab    for any iteration ends at the label \i !IGNORE!\i0  whether control\par
\tab\tab\tab    is transferred there explicitly or execution falls through\par
\tab\tab\tab    from the prior command.)\par
\par
Q1"EOPL8CH1$\tab    If the test character is a <NUL> (ASCII code 0), transfer\par
\tab\tab\tab    control to the portion of the program labeled \i !PL8CH1!\i0 .\par
\tab\tab\tab    Files created by OS/8 PAL8 must be checked for a potential\par
\tab\tab\tab    quirk associated with <NUL> characters near the end of the\par
\tab\tab\tab    file.  The <NUL> character will be removed; additional\par
\tab\tab\tab    output filtering may be performed.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    Note: Surprisingly, many OS/8 text files processed through\par
\tab\tab\tab    the PTP: handler contain significant quantities of <NUL>\par
\tab\tab\tab    characters typically in large groups.  Similar special cases\par
\tab\tab\tab    are known to exist which are handled elsewhere in this\par
                     program.\par
\page 'Q1-127"EOIGNORE$\tab    If the latest character is a <RO> (ASCII code 127 decimal,\par
\tab\tab\tab    177 octal]), it will be removed by transferring control\par
\tab\tab\tab    to the portion of the program labeled \i !IGNORE!\i0 ; the latest\par
\tab\tab\tab    character will not be placed into the output file.  Various\par
\tab\tab\tab    files are known to contain extraneous <RO> characters for a\par
\tab\tab\tab    variety of reasons.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    Note: Virtually all P?S/8 device 66 lineprinter output\par
\tab\tab\tab    starts with a single <RO> character.  This was implemented\par
\tab\tab\tab    based on misleading information suggesting this as the\par
\tab\tab\tab    proper technique to initialize the printer interface.\par
\tab\tab\tab    Future releases of P?S/8 will output a leading <NUL>\par
\tab\tab\tab    character to enhance compatibility with certain quirky\par
\tab\tab\tab    printers where a graphic character (such as a heart) is\par
\tab\tab\tab    printed when <RO> is transmitted.  Proper implementation will\par
                     allow a one-word patch to restore the historic value of <RO> \par
                     if desired.\par
\par
'OFFCHK$\tab\tab    Transfer control to the portion of the program labeled\par
\tab\tab\tab    \i !FFCHK!\i0  to handle potential <FF> character situations.\par
\par
\b Line 6\tab\tab    !PL8CH1!0A+1"NOIGNORE$'-7A-12"NOIGNORE$'-7DOIGNORE$\b0\par
\par
!PL8CH1!\tab\tab    A <NUL> character was detected in the output file.  If this\par
\tab\tab\tab    is the last character in the file, determine if the file was\par
\tab\tab\tab    created by PAL8 without the /C (CREF) option set.  In this\par
\tab\tab\tab    specific instance, PAL8 creates quirky output of several\par
\tab\tab\tab    blank lines after the last <FF> character with the first\par
\tab\tab\tab    line incompletely formed.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    This actually represents two different problems: a) The file\par
\tab\tab\tab    should end with a <FF> character (ignoring trailing <NUL>\par
\tab\tab\tab    characters which will be removed), b) The blank lines are\par
\tab\tab\tab    not formed correctly; one consists of merely a <LF>\par
\tab\tab\tab    character.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    When the /C option is passed to PAL8, a chain to CREF.SV\par
\tab\tab\tab    will be performed; the final output file will correctly end\par
\tab\tab\tab    with a <FF> character (ignoring trailing <NUL> characters\par
\tab\tab\tab    which will be removed).  The blank lines are still present\par
\tab\tab\tab    in the output file; STRIP.TEC includes programming to\par
\tab\tab\tab    correct defectively formed blank lines.  This will lead to a\par
\tab\tab\tab    deliberate blank page in the output consistent with the\par
\tab\tab\tab    original behavior of PAL8 when used with physical printers.\par
\par
                     Note: Post-processing of the output file may disallow blank\par
                     pages; disposition of blank pages is beyond the scope of this\par
                     program.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    Since a design goal of STRIP.TEC is to remove the last <FF>\par
\tab\tab\tab    character, the entire sequence will be removed.\par
\par
                     Note: The sequence of characters removed consists of <FF> <LF>\par
                     <CR> <LF> <CR> <LF>; the last character in the file is a <NUL>\par
\tab\tab\tab    character which will also be removed.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    Other than this quirky specific case, no properly formed file\par
\tab\tab\tab    should be modified.  Another portion of the program deals with\par
                     the a similar problem when <NUL> characters are not involved\par
                     should the file become available in the host environment by an\par
                     exceptional process that avoids the PTP: handler artifacts.\par
\par
0A+1"NOIGNORE$\tab    If the code returned for the next character following (0A)\par
\tab\tab\tab    the detected <NUL> character is -1, transfer control to the\par
\tab\tab\tab    portion of the program labeled \i !IGNORE!\i0  to remove the\par
\tab\tab\tab    latest <NUL> character.  This portion of the program is\par
\tab\tab\tab    concerned only with a <NUL> character that is at the end of\par
\tab\tab\tab    the file.\page '-7A-12"NOIGNORE$\tab    If the character before the previous five characters is not\par
\tab\tab\tab    a <FF> (ASCII code 12 decimal, 14 octal), transfer control\par
\tab\tab\tab    to the portion of the program labeled \i !IGNORE!\i0  to remove the\par
\tab\tab\tab    last <NUL> character in the file; the file does not have the\par
\tab\tab\tab    quirky special case present.\par
\par
'-7DOIGNORE$\tab    Delete the <FF> and all of the other characters through the\par
\tab\tab\tab    end of the file; transfer control to the portion of the\par
\tab\tab\tab    program labeled \i !IGNORE!\i0  to continue the iteration loop.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    Note: The next iteration will break out of the loop when\par
\tab\tab\tab    the nonstop search fails to complete this operation.\par
\par
\b Line 7\tab\tab    !FFCHK!Q1-12"NONOTFF$'0A&127-12"EOIGNORE$'QF"NOPASTBEG$\b0\par
\par
!FFCHK!\tab\tab    This portion of the program checks for <FF> characters.\par
\tab\tab\tab    This is a pivotal test as anomalies may be present either\par
\tab\tab\tab    just before or just after a <FF> character.  It is theorized\par
\tab\tab\tab    most of these problems stem from an incomplete understanding\par
\tab\tab\tab    of how to properly format logically distinct print pages\par
                     within various programs.  Conforming text files separate pages\par
                     with a single <FF> character; unfortunately, several anomalies\par
                     may be present. \par
\par
Q1-12"NONOTFF$\tab    If the latest character is not a <FF> (ASCII code 12 decimal,\par
                     14 octal), transfer control to the portion of the program\par
                     labeled \i !NOTFF!\i0 .\par
\par
'0A&127-12"EOIGNORE$ The latest character is a <FF>.  If the next character is also\par
                     a <FF> (ASCII code 12 decimal, 14 octal), transfer control to\par
\tab\tab\tab    the portion of the program labeled \i !IGNORE!\i0  (to ignore the\par
\tab\tab\tab    latest character).  The next iteration will handle repeat\par
\tab\tab\tab    situation such that a string of adjacent <FF> characters will\par
                     all be ignored.\par
\par
                     Note: The latest character must be .AND. 127 (177 octal) since\par
                     the latest character could have the parity bit set.\par
\par
'QF"NOPASTBEG$\tab    Q-register F is tested to determine if this section has been\par
\tab\tab\tab    executed previously.  If so, transfer control to the portion\par
\tab\tab\tab    of the program labeled \i !PASTBEG!\i0  to avoid repeat processing.\par
\par
