                     character are <FF> <CR> <LF> they will be removed.  Checking\par
                     three characters before the Control-Z sentinel character,\par
                     compare that character to <FF>.  If not a match, proceed to the\par
                     next section of the program (labeled \i !NOFF!\i0 ).\par
\par
                     Note: This simplified method is adequate for this purpose.  The\par
                     reason is that it is not possible for there to be a line that\par
                     does not have both a <CR> and a <LF> at this point in the\par
                     filtering process.  As such, the two characters after the <FF>\par
                     are already completely determined and all three characters can\par
                     be removed with impunity.\par
\par
.-3UZQZJ!            If the tested character matches <FF>, store it's position in Q\par
                     register Z; then change the edit buffer pointer to match this \par
                     position effectively ignoring the three undesirable characters.\par
                     (The label \i !NOFF!\i0  follows.)\par
\par
!NOFF!0,.P0,.K\tab    All characters in the edit buffer to this point (0,.) are\par
\tab\tab\tab    output using the Punch (P) command then deleted using the\par
\tab\tab\tab    Kill (K) command.  These operations are skipped if the edit\par
\tab\tab\tab    buffer is empty.  (The label \i !NOCHARS!\i0  follows.)\par
\par
!NOCHARS!EFEX$\tab    The output file is closed using the End File (EF) command;\par
\tab\tab\tab    TECO EXits (EX) to the operating system.\page\b Line #5:\tab\tab    !NOTEND!Q1"EOPL8CH1$'Q1-127"EOIGNORE$'OFFCHK$\b0\par
\par
!NOTEND!\tab\tab    The rest of the program processes the test character\par
\tab\tab\tab    checking for various special cases.  All additional\par
\tab\tab\tab    operations are within the iteration brackets ("<" and ">")\par
\tab\tab\tab    and will be performed repeatedly.  Each iteration starts\par
\tab\tab\tab    with the Non-stop search for any existent character until\par
\tab\tab\tab    failing due to lack of further input.  (Note: All processing\par
\tab\tab\tab    for any iteration ends at the label \i !IGNORE!\i0  whether control\par
\tab\tab\tab    is transferred there explicitly or execution falls through\par
\tab\tab\tab    from the prior command.)\par
\par
Q1"EOPL8CH1$\tab    If the test character is a <NUL> (ASCII code 0), transfer\par
\tab\tab\tab    control to the portion of the program labeled \i !PL8CH1!\i0 .\par
\tab\tab\tab    Files created by OS/8 PAL8 must be checked for a potential\par
\tab\tab\tab    quirk associated with <NUL> characters near the end of the\par
\tab\tab\tab    file.  The <NUL> character will be removed; additional\par
\tab\tab\tab    output filtering may be performed.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    Note: Surprisingly, many OS/8 text files processed through\par
\tab\tab\tab    the PTP: handler contain significant quantities of <NUL>\par
\tab\tab\tab    characters typically in large groups.  Similar special cases\par
\tab\tab\tab    are known to exist which are handled elsewhere in this program.\par
\par
'Q1-127"EOIGNORE$\tab    If the latest character is a <RO> (ASCII code 127 decimal,\par
\tab\tab\tab    177 octal]), it will be removed by transferring control\par
\tab\tab\tab    to the portion of the program labeled \i !IGNORE!\i0 ; the latest\par
\tab\tab\tab    character will not be placed into the output file.  Various\par
\tab\tab\tab    files are known to contain extraneous <RO> characters for a\par
\tab\tab\tab    variety of reasons.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    Note: Virtually all P?S/8 device 66 lineprinter output\par
\tab\tab\tab    starts with a single <RO> character.  This was implemented\par
\tab\tab\tab    based on misleading information suggesting this as the\par
\tab\tab\tab    proper technique to initialize the printer interface.\par
\tab\tab\tab    Future releases of P?S/8 will output a leading <NUL>\par
\tab\tab\tab    character to enhance compatibility with certain quirky\par
\tab\tab\tab    printers where a graphic character (such as a heart) is\par
\tab\tab\tab    printed when <RO> is transmitted.  Proper implementation will\par
                     allow a one-word patch to restore the historic value of <RO> if\par
\tab\tab\tab    desired.\par
\par
'OFFCHK$\tab\tab    Transfer control to the portion of the program labeled\par
\tab\tab\tab    \i !FFCHK!\i0  to handle potential <FF> character situations.\par
\par
\b Line #6:\tab\tab    !PL8CH1!0A+1"NOIGNORE$'-7A-12"NOIGNORE$'-7DOIGNORE$\b0\par
\par
!PL8CH1!\tab\tab    A <NUL> character was detected in the output file.  If this\par
\tab\tab\tab    is the last character in the file, determine if the file was\par
\tab\tab\tab    created by PAL8 without the /C (CREF) option set.  In this\par
\tab\tab\tab    specific instance, PAL8 creates quirky output of several\par
\tab\tab\tab    blank lines after the last <FF> character with the first\par
\tab\tab\tab    line incompletely formed.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    This actually represents two different problems: a) The file\par
\tab\tab\tab    should end with a <FF> character (ignoring trailing <NUL>\par
\tab\tab\tab    characters which will be removed), b) The blank lines are\par
\tab\tab\tab    not formed correctly; one consists of merely a <LF>\par
\tab\tab\tab    character.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    When the /C option is passed to PAL8, a chain to CREF.SV\par
\tab\tab\tab    will be performed; the final output file will correctly end\par
\tab\tab\tab    with a <FF> character (ignoring trailing <NUL> characters\par
\tab\tab\tab    which will be removed).  The blank lines are still present\par
\tab\tab\tab    in the output file; STRIP.TEC includes programming to\par
\tab\tab\tab    correct defectively formed blank lines.  This will lead to a\par
\tab\tab\tab    deliberate blank page in the output consistent with the\par
\tab\tab\tab    original behavior of PAL8 when used with physical printers.\page\tab\tab\tab    Note: Post-processing of the output file may disallow blank\par
                     pages; disposition of blank pages is beyond the scope of this\par
                     program.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    Since a design goal of STRIP.TEC is to remove the last <FF>\par
\tab\tab\tab    character, the entire sequence will be removed.\par
\par
                     Note: The sequence of characters removed consists of <FF> <LF>\par
                     <CR> <LF> <CR> <LF>; the last character in the file is a <NUL>\par
\tab\tab\tab    character which will also be removed.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    Other than this quirky specific case, no properly formed file\par
\tab\tab\tab    should be modified.  Another portion of the program deals with\par
                     the a similar problem when <NUL> characters are not involved\par
                     should the file become available in the host environment by an\par
                     exceptional process that avoids the PTP: handler artifacts.\par
\par
0A+1"NOIGNORE$\tab    If the code returned for the next character following (0A)\par
\tab\tab\tab    the detected <NUL> character is -1, transfer control to the\par
\tab\tab\tab    portion of the program labeled \i !IGNORE!\i0  to remove the\par
\tab\tab\tab    latest <NUL> character.  This portion of the program is\par
\tab\tab\tab    concerned only with a <NUL> character that is at the end of\par
\tab\tab\tab    the file.\par
\par
'-7A-12"NOIGNORE$\tab    If the character before the previous five characters is not\par
\tab\tab\tab    a <FF> (ASCII code 12 decimal, 14 octal), transfer control\par
\tab\tab\tab    to the portion of the program labeled \i !IGNORE!\i0  to remove the\par
\tab\tab\tab    last <NUL> character in the file; the file does not have the\par
\tab\tab\tab    quirky special case present.\par
\par
'-7DOIGNORE$\tab    Delete the <FF> and all of the other characters through the\par
\tab\tab\tab    end of the file; transfer control to the portion of the\par
\tab\tab\tab    program labeled \i !IGNORE!\i0  to continue the iteration loop.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    Note: The next iteration will break out of the loop when\par
\tab\tab\tab    the nonstop search fails to complete this operation.\par
\par
\b Line #7:\tab\tab    !FFCHK!Q1-12"NONOTFF$'0A&127-12"EOIGNORE$'QF"NOPASTBEG$\b0\par
\par
!FFCHK!\tab\tab    This portion of the program checks for <FF> characters.\par
\tab\tab\tab    This is a pivotal test as anomalies may be present either\par
\tab\tab\tab    just before or just after a <FF> character.  It is theorized\par
\tab\tab\tab    most of these problems stem from an incomplete understanding\par
\tab\tab\tab    of how to properly format logically distinct print pages\par
                     within various programs.  Conforming text files separate pages\par
                     with a single <FF> character; unfortunately, several anomalies\par
                     may be present. \par
\par
Q1-12"NONOTFF$\tab    If the latest character is not a <FF> (ASCII code 12 decimal,\par
                     14 octal), transfer control to the portion of the program\par
                     labeled \i !NOTFF!\i0 .\par
\par
'0A&127-12"EOIGNORE$ The latest character is a <FF>.  If the next character is also\par
                     a <FF> (ASCII code 12 decimal, 14 octal), transfer control to\par
\tab\tab\tab    the portion of the program labeled \i !IGNORE!\i0  (to ignore the\par
\tab\tab\tab    latest character).  The next iteration will handle repeat\par
\tab\tab\tab    situation such that a string of adjacent <FF> characters will\par
                     all be ignored.\par
\par
                     Note: The latest character must be .AND. 127 (177 octal) since\par
                     the latest character could have the parity bit set.\par
\par
'QF"NOPASTBEG$\tab    Q-register F is tested to determine if this section has been\par
\tab\tab\tab    executed previously.  If so, transfer control to the portion\par
\tab\tab\tab    of the program labeled \i !PASTBEG!\i0  to avoid repeat processing.\par
\par
\b Line #8:\tab\tab    '.-11"L0,.DOIGNORE$'!PASTBEG!%F'.USQH"EOIGNORE$\b0\par
\par
'.-11"L0,.DOIGNORE$' Since the <FF> character match is the first one encountered\par
\tab\tab\tab    in the file, it is possible there are a few characters just\par
\tab\tab\tab    before the <FF> which is generally the first character in\par
\tab\tab\tab    the file (assuming it is at the beginning of the file at\par
\tab\tab\tab    all).  However, certain programs output small groups of\par
                     various characters before the <FF> to ensure the viability of\par
                     the lineprinter interface.  If this is the case, all of these\par
\tab\tab\tab    characters will be deleted.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    Transfer control to the portion of the program labeled\par
\tab\tab\tab    \i !IGNORE!\i0  to continue processing; initial <FF> characters are\par
\tab\tab\tab    removed.\par
\par
                     Note: This secion can only be executed once; Q-Register F is\par
                     incremented as necessary to prevent this section from being\par
                     executed again.  See the previous section above for more\par
                     information.\par
\par
!PASTBEG!%F\tab\tab    Control is transferred to this portion of the program to\par
\tab\tab\tab    ensure the previous once-only section is not executed again.\par
                     Q-register F is incremented accordingly.\par
\par
.US\tab\tab\tab    The current edit buffer position (.) is stored (U) in\par
\tab\tab\tab    numeric Q-register S.  Certain operations may be performed\par
\tab\tab\tab    on characters located past the current position; after these\par
\tab\tab\tab    operations complete, the buffer pointer can be restored\par
\tab\tab\tab    using the contents of Q-register S that was stored at this\par
\tab\tab\tab    earlier point in the program execution.\par
\par
QH"EOIGNORE$'\tab    As a special accommodation for output files created by PAL8\par
\tab\tab\tab    and related OS/8 utilities, if the first character in the\par
\tab\tab\tab    file is a <FF> character (after first removing all leading\par
\tab\tab\tab    <NUL> and <RO> characters), it will be removed.  Additional\par
\tab\tab\tab    <NUL> or <RO> characters may follow; removal of these\par
                     undesirable artifact characters is accomplished elsewhere in\par
                     the program.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    Notes:\par
\par
                     1) When output files from PAL8 and related OS/8 utilities are\par
                        processed by the device 66 LPT: handler, additional leading\par
                        <FF> character(s) may be inserted by the device handler.\par
                        The program logic is designed to eliminate any number of\par
                        leading <FF> characters until a printing character is\par
                        processed (this includes <CR> or <LF> but not undersirable\par
                        artifact characters).\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    2) P?S/8 PAL and related utilities (perhaps only from the\par
                        circa 1978 release o P?S/8 Version 8T) do not conform to\par
                        this convention; the first <FF> (and any prior <CR> and\par
                        <LF> characters) will be removed in another section of the\par
                        program.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    3) This program depends on the known behavior of TECOC for\par
                        MS-DOS and Windows to initialize numeric Q-register storage \par
                        to zero.  This behavior is often implementation-specific\par
                        and a general TECO standard feature.  If the TECO\par
                        implementation used supports errors on uninitialized\par
                        variables, the program should be modified to initialize\par
                        variables using commands such as OUH.  (Other TECO features\par
                        used are virtually specific to TECOC.)\par
\par
\b Line #9:\tab\tab    $'0A+1"EOIGNORE$'2A+1"NOPL8CH2$\b0\par
\par
0A+1"EOIGNORE$\tab    STRIP.TEC attempts to remove trailing <FF> characters if\par
\tab\tab\tab    possible; text files are handled differently in the SIMH\par
\tab\tab\tab    host environment from classic direct printing techniques on\par
\tab\tab\tab    physical printing hardware; post-processing is generally\par
\tab\tab\tab    applied before final printing (or further conversion for\par
\tab\tab\tab    publishing purposes).  As such, steps must be taken to avoid\par
\tab\tab\tab    trailing blank pages.\page\tab\tab\tab    TECO implements safeguards associated with the end of file\par
\tab\tab\tab    condition.  Attempts to access additional characters will\par
\tab\tab\tab    result in an error abort.  To avoid this situation, if the\par
\tab\tab\tab    code returned for the next character following (0A) (the\par
\tab\tab\tab    <FF> character) is -1, the end of file has been reached.\par
\tab\tab\tab    Control will be transferred to the portion of the program\par
\tab\tab\tab    labeled \i !IGNORE!\i0  to avoid processing the trailing <FF>\par
\tab\tab\tab    character; the next iteration will cause the search to fail\par
\tab\tab\tab    and break out of the iteration loop to allow proper file\par
\tab\tab\tab    closing and exit.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    Note: In the unlikely event a file contains alternate\par
\tab\tab\tab    quirky output anomalies not quite identical to the condition\par
\tab\tab\tab    checked for here, the test for this condition will not\par
\tab\tab\tab    detect the actual problem present; should this occur, a\par
\tab\tab\tab    viable remedy is to run the STRIP.TEC program a second (and\par
\tab\tab\tab    perhaps even a third) time.  On reprocessed files, existing\par
\tab\tab\tab    tests should successfully remove the trailing <FF>.)  (Note:\par
\tab\tab\tab    Before this feature was added to STRIP.TEC, removal of this\par
\tab\tab\tab    specific quirk of PAL8 required a two-pass approach.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    If the end of file has not been reached, program flow\par
\tab\tab\tab    continues.\par
\par
'2A+1"NOPL8CH2$\tab    If the buffer pointer is not at the end of file, check if\par
\tab\tab\tab    the end of file is after exactly two additional characters\par
\tab\tab\tab    (2A).  (Note: The most likely case of extraneous output is\par
\tab\tab\tab    <CR> followed by <LF> after the (otherwise) trailing <FF>\par
\tab\tab\tab    character.)  In general, this condition does not apply.\par
\tab\tab\tab    Transfer control to the portion of the program labeled\par
\tab\tab\tab    \i !PL8CH2!\i0 .\par
\par
\b Line #10:\tab\tab    '2DOIGNORE$!PL8CH2!6A+1"NOFRSTCHK$\b0\par
\par
'2DOIGNORE$\tab\tab    Remove the two characters past the (otherwise) trailing <FF>\par
\tab\tab\tab    character (2D) .  Then transfer control to the portion of the\par
                     program labeled \i !IGNORE!\i0 ; the trailing <FF> character will be\par
                     removed.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    Note: Future releases of STRIP.TEC may reorganize this program\par
                     section.\par
\par
!PL8CH2!\tab\tab    This portion of the program also checks for the quirky output\par
                     of PAL8 when the /C (CREF) option is not used.  The same logic\par
                     is used with the exception of the <NUL> character\par
                     consideration.  Files that are present in the host environment\par
                     transmitted by means other than the PTP: handler may still have\par
                     the same quirky output.\par
\par
                     Note: Files processed by prior versions of STRIP.TEC may\par
                     require processing by this (newer) release.\par
\par
6A+1"NOFRSTCHK$\tab    If the end of file is not after exactly six additional\par
                     characters, transfer control to the portion of the program\par
                     labeled \i !FRSTCHK!\i0 .\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    If the end of file is detected after exactly six additional\par
                     characters, program flow continues on the next line following.\par
\par
\b Line #11:          '6DOIGNORE$!FRSTCHK!QP"NOLOOP1$\b0\par
\par
'6DOIGNORE$          Delete the six additional characters.  Transfer control to the\par
                     portion of the program labeled \i !IGNORE!\i0 ; the trailing <FF>\par
                     character will be removed.\page !FRSTCHK!\tab\tab    This is the section of the program labeled \i !FRSTCHK!\i0 .  When the\par
                     program continues here, the portion of the edit buffer being\par
                     processed is not at the end of the file.  As such, initial <FF>\par
                     checking (when a <FF> is not the first character in the file)\par
                     can be checked at this point.\par
\par
QP"NOLOOP1$\tab\tab    If this is not the first <FF> encountered (because the contents\par
                     of Q-register P is non-zero), transfer control to the portion\par
                     of the program labeled \i !LOOP1!\i0 .  Only the initial detected <FF>\par
                     character is considered in this section.\par
\par
                     Note: This test is required for files where the first <FF> is\par
                     not the first character in the file (but only a few additional\par
                     characters precede the first <FF> character which should be\par
                     removed).\par
\par
Line #12:\tab\tab    \b '%P$.-4"NOLOOP1$'-4DOIGNORE$\b0\par
\par
'%P$.-4"NOLOOP1$\tab    Increment (%) the contents of Q-register P to prevent\par
                     additional initial <FF> testing in later program iterations.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    Note: Certain printing files created by the circa 1978 release\par
                     of P?S/8 (Version 8T) are known to start the output with a\par
                     small number (four) of introductory characters prior to the\par
                     first <FF> character.  If this is not the case, transfer\par
                     control to the portion of the program labeled \i !LOOP1!\i0 .\par
\par
'-4DOIGNORE$\tab    If the introductory characters are present before the current\par
                     edit buffer position, delete (D) the first four characters\par
                     (-4).  Transfer control to the portion of the program labeled\par
                     \i !IGNORE!\i0  to remove the initial <FF> character.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    Note: Newer releases of P?S/8 eliminated this problem; only\par
                     output oriented to console terminals should have initial\par
                     <CR>/<LF>-only lines before the first printing page (and only\par
                     if the proper command switches are used to produce page-\par
                     oriented output with tear-off lines).\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    When this portion of certain P?S/8 programs was designed,\par
                     handling of physical lineprinters was not well documented.\par
                     Later releases of P?S/8 generally do not exhibit this quirk in\par
                     P?S/8 standard system programs (but may have alternate quirks\par
                     handled elsewhere in this program).  Certain user programs may\par
                     exist that copied the older method; STRIP.TEC will correctly\par
                     filter the output of all such programs by the logic implemented\par
                     in this section.\par
